#{[17592186304947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304947 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "050795d3-56ec-5162-9d13-5f770f4a560b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304947 #edb/ref 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that  $L\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e98f8075-941a-5e1c-9a37-f46cd62e24a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304950 #edb/ref 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a494f0da-488f-5d2c-a912-87d73ea366c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304979 #edb/ref 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{5t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75147345-f194-5790-8fe4-ff587f8030d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304995 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d34dedd9-0917-526a-8fa5-cdd60c269ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305000 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{6{e}^{2t}\\}+L\\{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}\\} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =6L\\{{e}^{2t}\\}+2L\\{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355332 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{s-2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4aca0833-9adf-5c73-a1ab-d07568183f99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355812 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s-2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355812 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355812 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8290279a-5ab8-57ac-9792-73f13640f201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355926 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{(s+2)(s-2)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8f0198a4-4fab-5362-bb17-7ad99d588499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355953 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{(s+2)^{2}(s-2)}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355953 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355953 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "91c23727-f24d-5c74-bf75-ea326ecfa263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356485 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356485 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356485 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d05463d4-fa4b-567c-9712-d946fd6321f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186355953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186356485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186356485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186356969 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1e1a231c-22ee-57a9-969d-ae5213d3dae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-331799" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a function $q(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{q(t)\\}=Q(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9cd6f01-d8c7-5536-9f5a-869210d50728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368062 #edb/ref 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{9t}q(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Q(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-9)t}\\,q(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Q(s-9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368063 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cf5bb005-5f3f-5af5-89f2-8ea2fb3c315f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368064 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(s)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fb8a4d5f-9a6d-54d5-8678-e59f13f43d9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368065 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(s-9)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368065 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368065 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd2db7c3-4056-5c0c-b123-027cb0e405d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368066 #edb/ref 1080 "Q(s+9)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d291efb0-d3c6-5c84-917b-6a21af29479d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1077 "field-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368067 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592