86368080 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368080 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bda30ae-8042-58bd-8658-26b8557e0ed0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332104" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2acfbcc3-d517-5f68-a1bd-d612fba90c87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368081 #edb/ref 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-2t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $2$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368082 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "84509ec0-1233-5211-bf4c-8b7806b1f61b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368083 #edb/ref 1080 "images/8d/8daba7e586feeff02624e5d0fb508c9d2adedb23d5a79fe329f83a42e1f8256e.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368083 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368083 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ebfe2578-7c5f-5df7-9d72-39a56be2386e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368084 #edb/ref 1080 "images/82/82d01729627b5871739120d2e512cf54610eaaf43f6f2f2c454b7e39803937a2.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368084 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368084 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e2c1dfa-8219-593c-b78b-6240e600685c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368085 #edb/ref 1080 "images/e6/e6d497165f1dbc9fd9db77e6a8943f6acd45262cb74ebafd4a418d612be884b7.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368085 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368085 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d7c5a3d-eb7d-5081-ba8b-a26f602f1890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368086 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a9/a902bac22ba86af8de8b49d5aba5bb34f35433230f303432e1f4327708f41a7d.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "134ca311-f8fb-5d46-8687-e4802cae00a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368087 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ef/ef457f3f2030bd9399e707ea5bc32f9fe58aae29f848c270c5b971afd98315bd.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368087 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368087 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "63430f1f-817b-5c2a-bd77-427ce4f138a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186368096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)={e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20b395e8-6914-5e1c-ade5-fc76b7cc76a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368095 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}+5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+L\\{5{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-4t}\\}+5L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+5\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{10}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368096 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368100 #edb/ref 17592186045